From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ognian Mitzev <OgiMitzev@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 23 May 2026 00:48:16 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] P09.56: MSVC vtable + RTTI emission
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Routes `emit_class_metadata` through an MSVC-specific path when
`target.is_like_msvc` is set. The Itanium path is unchanged.

MSVC emission diverges in three ways from Itanium:

1. **No offset-to-top header in the vftable.** Itanium puts
   offset-to-top at slot 0 and `_ZTI` ptr at slot 1, with the
   address point at slot 2. MSVC puts the Complete Object
   Locator (COL) pointer at vftable[-1] and the address point
   sits on the first function pointer. We emit
   `??_7Class@@6B@ = { COL_ptr, fn1, fn2, ... }` and the
   vptr-init path's GEP offset becomes `1 * ptr_bytes` instead
   of `2 * ptr_bytes`.

2. **RTTI is a tree of small globals, not `_ZTI`/`_ZTS`.** The
   Type Descriptor (`??_R0?AV<class>@@@8`) carries a runtime-
   filled vptr + spare + `.?AV<class>@@`-decorated name string.
   The Complete Object Locator (`??_R4<class>@@6B<class>@@@`)
   references the Type Descriptor + a Class Hierarchy Descriptor
   slot (currently null — extended hierarchies follow in a
   later patch). The full R0/R1/R2/R3/R4 tree lands when
   multi-inheritance / virtual-base support gets exercised.

3. **Dtor slot routing** (intentionally left for the next patch
   in the series — P09.57). For now the vftable dtor slot
   carries the base dtor (`??1Class@@`) rather than the scalar
   deleting destructor (`??_GClass@@`); `delete this` paths
   will be wrong until P09.57 lands.

The mangling itself routes correctly via the AbiFlavor
dispatcher introduced in P09.55 — `mangle_cxx_vtable_symbol`,
`mangle_cxx_typeinfo_symbol`, `mangle_cxx_typeinfo_name_symbol`
all produce MSVC-flavored symbols on `is_like_msvc` targets
with no call-site changes needed in this patch.

In-tree lints suppressed in `rustc_abi_cxx/lib.rs`:
`rustc::default_hash_types` (vendored crate uses std HashMap,
not FxHashMap; no global rustc state to instabilize),
`rustc::potential_query_instability` (same reason), and
`unexpected_cfgs` (the `serde` feature is workspace-only).

Validated with `./x.py check --stage 1 compiler/rustc_codegen_llvm` —
full type-check passes including the new MSVC emission path.
---
 compiler/rustc_abi_cxx/src/lib.rs             |  11 +
 compiler/rustc_codegen_llvm/src/cxx_vtable.rs | 213 +++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 221 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/compiler/rustc_abi_cxx/src/lib.rs b/compiler/rustc_abi_cxx/src/lib.rs
index 9c048a1406e..0c43c1c385f 100644
--- a/compiler/rustc_abi_cxx/src/lib.rs
+++ b/compiler/rustc_abi_cxx/src/lib.rs
@@ -8,6 +8,17 @@
 
 #![deny(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]
 #![allow(dead_code)]
+// Vendored from the rustcc workspace — uses `std::collections::HashMap`
+// internally (not `FxHashMap`). The crate has no global state shared
+// with rustc's query system, so query-instability concerns don't
+// apply. Silence both lints here.
+#![allow(rustc::default_hash_types)]
+#![allow(rustc::potential_query_instability)]
+// `cfg_attr(feature = "serde", ...)` is unused inside the rustc tree
+// (the vendored crate carries it for the standalone workspace build
+// where the cache layer needs serde). In-tree Cargo.toml has no
+// `serde` feature, so these become a no-op — silence the lint.
+#![allow(unexpected_cfgs)]
 
 mod ctx;
 mod diag;
diff --git a/compiler/rustc_codegen_llvm/src/cxx_vtable.rs b/compiler/rustc_codegen_llvm/src/cxx_vtable.rs
index 027536007da..d2689b014bb 100644
--- a/compiler/rustc_codegen_llvm/src/cxx_vtable.rs
+++ b/compiler/rustc_codegen_llvm/src/cxx_vtable.rs
@@ -82,10 +82,16 @@ pub(crate) fn maybe_emit_vptr_init<'ll, 'tcx>(
     let vtable_base = bx.cx.declare_global(&vtable_sym, ptr_ty);
 
     // The address point is at byte offset `2 * ptr_bytes` from
-    // the vtable's start (skipping offset-to-top and the RTTI
-    // pointer, per the Itanium single-inheritance convention).
+    // the vtable's start under Itanium (skipping offset-to-top
+    // and the RTTI pointer). Under MSVC the vftable has no
+    // header at all from the address point's perspective — the
+    // COL sits at conceptual vftable[-1]. In our emitted MSVC
